This paper investigates the existence of solutions to the following boundary value problem defined on a complete manifold $M$:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
-\Delta u + \frac{a}{u^{\alpha}} + b|u|^{p-2}u = \lambda u + f(x,u) & \text{in } M, \\
u > 0 & \text{in } M,
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
where $a, b, \lambda$ are positive constants, $0 < \alpha < p$, and $f$ is a nonnegative function. The differential operator $-\Delta$ is understood as the Laplace-Beltrami operator on $M$. We establish the solvability of this problem under the assumption that the manifold $M$ satisfies the $\lambda_1$-Poincaré inequality.

This study addresses the estimation of functions within the Hankel Sobolev space $H^{
u}_{s}$ ($
u > 0$) using Prolate Spheroidal Wave Functions (P.S.W.F.s). We begin by deriving the P.S.W.F. expansion for functions in $H^{
u}_{s}$, followed by establishing a corresponding discrete orthogonality relation for these P.S.W.F.s. Subsequently, we apply the P.S.W.F. method to construct a specific estimator and derive an upper bound for its mean square error.
